1. DISTINCT

Used to remove duplicate values/rows from the selected result.

SELECT DISTINCT department
FROM students;

Example:

CSE
ECE
CSE
IT

becomes:

CSE
ECE
IT
With multiple columns
SELECT DISTINCT department, age
FROM students;

DISTINCT checks the combination of selected columns.

2. NULL

NULL means missing / unknown value.

It is NOT the same as:

0       → zero
''      → empty string
'NULL'  → text "NULL"
NULL    → missing/unknown
Check for NULL

❌ Don't use:

WHERE phone = NULL;

✅ Use:

SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE phone IS NULL;
Check for non-NULL
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE phone IS NOT NULL;

You can insert:

INSERT INTO students
VALUES (1, 'Brabu', 21, NULL, 'CSE');

Here:

id	name	age	phone	department
1	Brabu	21	NULL	CSE

So phone has no value.

2. Important: Don't use quotes ❌
'NULL'

This is not NULL. It is just the text "NULL".

3. Aggregate Functions

Used to perform calculations on multiple rows.

COUNT() → count
SUM()   → total
AVG()   → average
MIN()   → smallest
MAX()   → largest
COUNT()
SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM employees;
COUNT(*)      → counts all rows
COUNT(column) → counts non-NULL values
SUM()
SELECT SUM(salary)
FROM employees;

→ Total salary.

AVG()
